<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-III-partial_derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Partial Derivatives</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> 
      Partial derivatives are a fundamental concept in multivariable calculus, allowing us to analyze how 
      functions change with respect to one variable while keeping others constant. In this chapter, we will 
      explore the definition of partial derivatives, techniques for computing them, and their applications in 
      various fields such as physics, engineering, and economics. 
      We will also discuss higher-order partial derivatives and the conditions under which mixed partial derivatives are equal.   
       </p>
        <p>
  A function <m>f</m> of two variables takes each number pair <m>(x, y)</m> from a set <m>D</m> 
  and gives a unique real number denoted by <m>f(x, y)</m>. The set <m>D</m> is the <term>domain</term> of <m>f</m> 
  and its <term>range</term> is the set of values that <m>f</m> takes on, that is,
  <md>\{ f(x, y) \mid (x, y) \in D \}.</md>
</p>

<p>
  For a function <m>z = f(x, y)</m>, the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with 
    respect to <m>x</m> at a point <m>(a, b)</m> is defined as:
    <md>
        f_{x}(a, b) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(a, b) = \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(a + h, b) - f(a, b)}{h}
    </md>
    provided this limit exists.
    Likewise, the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>y</m> at
    <m>(a, b)</m> is:
    <md>
        f_{y}(a, b) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(a, b) = \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(a, b + h) - f(a, b)}{h}
    </md>
    For a general point <m>(x, y)</m>, replacing <m>(a, b)</m> with <m>(x, y)</m>
    gives the partial derivative functions <m>f_{x}(x, y)</m> and
    <m>f_{y}(x, y)</m>.
    For a function of <m>n</m> variables <m>f(x_{1}, x_{2}, \dots, x_{n})</m>,
    <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{i}}</m> is defined by changing only
    <m>x_{i}</m> by <m>h</m> and keeping all other <m>x_{j}</m> (<m>j \neq i</m>)
    fixed.
</p>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the domain and range of <m>f(x, y) = \sqrt{9 - x^2 - y^2}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The domain of <m>f</m> is the set of all points <m>(x, y)</m> such that the expression under the square root is non-negative:
      <md>
        9 - x^2 - y^2 \geq 0 \implies x^2 + y^2 \leq 9
      </md>
      This describes a disk of radius 3 centered at the origin in the xy-plane. Therefore, the domain is:
      <md>
        D = \{(x, y) \mid x^2 + y^2 \leq 9\}
      </md>
      The range of <m>f</m> is determined by the values that <m>f(x, y)</m> can take. 
      Since the maximum value occurs when <m>x = 0</m> and <m>y = 0</m>, we have:
      <md>
        f(0, 0) = \sqrt{9 - 0 - 0} = 3
      </md>
      The minimum value occurs when <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>, which gives:
      <md>
        f(x, y) = \sqrt{9 - 9} = 0
      </md>
      Therefore, the range of <m>f</m> is:
      <md>
        R = [0, 3]
      </md> 
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the domain and range and sketch the graph of <m>h(x, y) = 4x^2 + y^2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The domain of <m>h</m> is the set of all points <m>(x, y)</m> in the xy-plane, since the expression is defined for all real values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Therefore, the domain is:
        <md>
          D = \{(x, y) \mid x, y \in \mathbb{R}\}
        </md>
        The range of <m>h</m> is determined by the values that <m>h(x, y)</m> can take. Since both terms are non-negative, the minimum value occurs when <m>x = 0</m> and <m>y = 0</m>, giving:
        <md>
          h(0, 0) = 4(0)^2 + (0)^2 = 0
        </md>
        As <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> increase in magnitude, <m>h(x, y)</m> increases without bound. Therefore, the range of <m>h</m> is:
        <md>
          R = [0, \infty)
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
        

  </introduction>

